Ignore single-letter scratch files

Command output was being captured to single-letter .txt files and those
were being committed, so a commit would remove a file nobody wanted rather
than change anything. They are transient by construction - created to read
one result, deleted immediately after - so they belong in .gitignore.

`aar` is the package, not a log, and nothing in the project is named to
collide with `?.txt`, which is why a one-character pattern is safe here
rather than a footgun.
